---
import DiscoveryLayout from '../../../components/DiscoveryLayout.astro';
import JourneyCards from '../../../components/JourneyCards.astro';
import '../../../styles/photo-measurement.css';
---
<DiscoveryLayout title="Photo Measurement Lab | WorkshopGirl" description="Measure and annotate workshop photos locally. Calibrate a reference, correct a known rectangular plane, measure distances, angles, paths, areas and circles, then export PNG and JSON." canonical="https://workshopgirl.com/tools/photo-measurement/">
  <a href="/tools/#measure-build">← Measure &amp; Build</a><p class="eyebrow">WorkshopGirl / local photo tools</p><h1>Photo Measurement <span>Lab.</span></h1><p class="lead">A known reference turns a workshop photograph into a useful estimate. Mark dimensions, compare shapes and keep a record of your work.</p>
  <p class="privacy">Images stay in this browser tab. No uploads, accounts or saved browser records. Export your work before closing or reloading.</p>
  <div id="photo-lab" class="photo-lab" aria-busy="false">
    <div class="photo-open"><label for="photo-file">Open image / photo library<input id="photo-file" type="file" accept="image/jpeg,image/png,image/webp" /></label><p>Or drop an image anywhere in the lab. JPEG, PNG, supported WebP · 20 MiB · 32 megapixels · 12,000 px per edge. Working image: at most 4 megapixels / 2,400 px per edge.</p></div>
    <p id="photo-image-info">No image open.</p><p id="photo-status" role="status" aria-live="polite">Open a photograph to begin.</p>
    <div class="photo-controls">
      <label>Tool<select id="photo-mode" disabled><option value="reference">Reference scale</option><option value="rectify">Perspective corners</option><option value="distance">Distance</option><option value="angle">Angle (second point = vertex)</option><option value="polyline">Polyline total</option><option value="area">Polygon area</option><option value="circle">Circle (center + edge)</option><option value="line">Annotation line</option><option value="arrow">Annotation arrow</option><option value="text">Text</option><option value="select">Select / move</option><option value="pan">Pan</option></select></label>
      <label>Display units<select id="photo-unit"><option>mm</option><option>cm</option><option>m</option><option>inch</option></select></label>
      <label>Label / annotation<input id="photo-label" type="text" maxlength="120" placeholder="Optional dimension label" /></label>
    </div>
    <div class="photo-workspace">
      <div class="photo-view"><div class="photo-toolbar"><button id="photo-zoom-in" data-needs-image disabled aria-label="Zoom in">+</button><button id="photo-zoom-out" data-needs-image disabled aria-label="Zoom out">−</button><output id="photo-zoom-value">100%</output><button id="photo-fit" data-needs-image disabled>Reset view</button><button id="photo-undo" disabled>Undo</button><button id="photo-redo" disabled>Redo</button><button id="photo-delete" disabled>Delete selected</button></div>
        <div class="photo-stage"><canvas id="photo-canvas" tabindex="0" aria-label="Photo measurement canvas" aria-describedby="photo-canvas-help">Photo measurement needs a browser with Canvas support. Use the coordinate controls and text measurement list.</canvas><p id="photo-empty">Open a photo.<br />Calibrate a reference.<br />Measure your work.</p></div>
        <p id="photo-canvas-help">Wheel / pinch to zoom. Choose Pan to drag the view. In Select, drag handles or lines; arrow keys move a selected point (Shift = 10 px). Enter finishes a path or applies a reference; Escape cancels; Delete removes the selection; Ctrl/⌘ Z undoes (Shift redoes). Visual point identification requires sight; exact coordinate controls and a text list are available below.</p>
        <p id="photo-pending">No unfinished points.</p><div class="photo-toolbar"><button id="photo-finish" disabled>Finish path</button><button id="photo-cancel" disabled>Cancel points / processing</button></div>
      </div>
      <aside class="photo-settings" aria-label="Calibration and perspective">
        <h2>1. Set the scale</h2><p>Pick two points on a reliable reference in the same plane as the object.</p><label>Known reference distance<input id="photo-known" type="number" min="0.000001" step="any" value="10" /></label><label>Reference unit<select id="photo-reference-unit"><option>mm</option><option>cm</option><option>m</option><option>inch</option></select></label><button id="photo-reference" disabled>Apply reference</button><p id="photo-calibration">Uncalibrated.</p><button id="photo-clear-scale" disabled>Clear calibration</button>
        <details><summary>2. Correct perspective (optional)</summary><p>Use only a flat rectangular surface of known real width and height. Choose Perspective corners, then top left → top right → bottom right → bottom left. A quadrilateral alone does not establish the correct aspect ratio.</p><label>Surface width<input id="photo-surface-width" type="number" min="0.000001" step="any" value="100" /></label><label>Surface height<input id="photo-surface-height" type="number" min="0.000001" step="any" value="100" /></label><label>Surface unit<select id="photo-surface-unit"><option>mm</option><option>cm</option><option>m</option><option>inch</option></select></label><p>Apply crops to this plane, clears existing measurements, annotations, reference and undo history, and sets scale from your dimensions. Notes remain. Only one correction per original image; restore the original to start again.</p><button id="photo-correct" disabled>Apply perspective correction</button></details>
      </aside>
    </div>
    <details class="photo-coordinates"><summary>Exact image coordinates / keyboard placement</summary><p>Coordinates use the full working image, independent of the visible zoom. The origin is its top left.</p><div class="photo-controls"><label>New point X<input id="photo-point-x" type="number" min="0" step="any" value="0" /></label><label>New point Y<input id="photo-point-y" type="number" min="0" step="any" value="0" /></label><button id="photo-add-point" data-needs-image disabled>Add point to active tool</button></div></details>
    <div id="photo-selection" hidden><h2>Edit selected item</h2><div class="photo-controls"><label>Selected handle<select id="photo-handle"></select></label><label>Selected point X<input id="photo-x" type="number" step="any" /></label><label>Selected point Y<input id="photo-y" type="number" step="any" /></label><button id="photo-apply-point">Apply point coordinates</button><label>Selected label<input id="photo-selected-label" maxlength="120" /></label><button id="photo-apply-label">Apply label</button></div></div>
    <h2>Measurements &amp; annotations</h2><p id="photo-empty-list">No measurements yet. Calibrate, choose a tool, then place points.</p><ul id="photo-list" class="photo-list" aria-label="Measurements and annotations"></ul>
    <label>Report notes<textarea id="photo-notes" rows="3" maxlength="2000" placeholder="Reference accuracy, setup, repeat measurements…"></textarea></label>
    <div class="photo-toolbar photo-export"><button id="photo-png" data-needs-image disabled>Export annotated PNG</button><button id="photo-json" data-needs-image disabled>Export measurement JSON</button><button id="photo-original" data-needs-image disabled>Restore original / clear edits</button><button id="photo-clear" data-needs-image disabled>Close image</button></div>
    <p>PNG contains the complete working image, independent of zoom, with labels and dimension values. JSON v1 contains geometry, original oriented coordinates, scale, measurements, units and notes. It excludes image bytes. Downloads are local; this tool cannot restore a session from JSON.</p>
  </div>
  <section class="photo-guide" aria-labelledby="photo-guide-heading"><h2 id="photo-guide-heading">A useful estimate starts with a good photograph.</h2><ol><li>Keep the camera square to a flat surface. Place an accurate ruler or known rectangle next to the object in the same plane. Fill the frame, keep it sharp, and avoid wide-angle distortion.</li><li>Use a long reference, with visible endpoints. Zoom to place points and repeat placements to check the spread. For example, ±1 pixel at each endpoint on a 100-pixel reference can already contribute about 2% scale uncertainty; displayed decimal places do not prove accuracy.</li><li>For perspective correction, supply both real dimensions of a rectangle. Correction cannot recover depth, lens distortion, curved surfaces, hidden edges or lost image detail. Distances and areas only apply to that plane; circles should be measured after correction when the plane is tilted.</li><li>Angles need three points, with the vertex second. Polyline and area need Finish path. A circle uses the center and radius point, not automatic detection or a fit to blurred edges.</li><li>Add labels and notes, then export PNG and JSON. Cross-check critical cuts, tolerances and safety-related dimensions with a physical instrument. This is observational documentation, not certified metrology.</li></ol><p>Privacy: image decode, correction, drawing and export happen locally. The site still loads normal site assets. This tab holds the image in memory; closing, restoring or replacing it releases processing resources. Reloading loses your edits.</p></section>
  <JourneyCards />
</DiscoveryLayout>
<script>import '../../../components/photo/controller.ts';</script>
